introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do we build software that doesn't break?"
  overview: "This unit takes you from writing simple classes to designing <strong>Professional Systems</strong>. You will learn how to use <strong>Inheritance</strong> to reuse code, <strong>Polymorphism</strong> to create flexible logic, and <strong>Composition</strong> to build complex objects from smaller parts."
  journey: "You'll start by mapping relationships (Is-A vs Has-A). Then you'll implement <strong>Inheritance Hierarchies</strong> and master <strong>Method Overriding</strong> to change behavior dynamically. Finally, you'll use <strong>Abstract Classes</strong> to define strict blueprints for your code."

concepts:
  - title: "Inheritance (Is-A)"
    icon: "fa-sitemap"
    content: |
      <p>Don't Repeat Yourself (DRY). Inheritance allows a child class to steal code from a parent.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Parent (Superclass):</strong> The generic version (e.g., Vehicle).</li>
          <li><strong>Child (Subclass):</strong> The specific version (e.g., Car). It gets all the variables and methods for free.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Polymorphism"
    icon: "fa-shapes"
    content: |
      <p>Treating different objects the same way. The code adapts at runtime.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Overriding:</strong> A child class replaces a parent's method with its own version.</li>
          <li><strong>Dynamic Dispatch:</strong> The program decides which version to run while it is running.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Composition (Has-A)"
    icon: "fa-puzzle-piece"
    content: |
      <p>Building complex objects by gluing simple ones together.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Composition:</strong> A Car <em>has a</em> Engine. If you destroy the Car, the Engine goes too.</li>
          <li><strong>Aggregation:</strong> A Library <em>has a</em> Book. If you close the Library, the Book still exists.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Abstraction"
    icon: "fa-box-open"
    content: |
      <p>Hiding complex details behind a clean interface.</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Abstract Class:</strong> A blueprint that cannot be created. It just defines what children MUST do.</li>
          <li><strong>Interfaces:</strong> Strictly defines a "contract" of methods.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Inheritance"
    definition: "A mechanism where a new class derives attributes and methods from an existing class."
  - term: "Polymorphism"
    definition: "The ability of different classes to be treated as instances of the same general class through inheritance."
  - term: "Method Overriding"
    definition: "Providing a specific implementation of a method that is already provided by the parent class."
  - term: "Abstract Class"
    definition: "A class that cannot be instantiated and is used to define a common interface for subclasses."
  - term: "Composition"
    definition: "A 'part-of' relationship where the child object's lifecycle is dependent on the parent."
  - term: "Aggregation"
    definition: "A 'has-a' relationship where the child object can exist independently of the parent."
  - term: "UML (Unified Modeling Language)"
    definition: "A standard visual modeling language used to visualize, specify, construct, and document software systems."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Overloading vs Overriding"
    correction: "Overloading = Same name, different parameters (compile time). Overriding = Same name, same parameters, different logic (runtime)."
  - mistake: "Inheritance is always best"
    correction: "Novices overuse inheritance. Often 'Composition' (keeping objects separate) is cleaner and less fragile."
  - mistake: "Abstract Classes are broken"
    correction: "Thinking because you can't satisfy 'new AbstractClass()', it's broken. It's meant to be a template only!"

